 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "Your city has a million people, and every well in town is drying up.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So you dam a river in the hills and run one big pipe into town.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "But the houses at the far end only get a trickle.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Water loses pressure as it travels, and every tap along the way takes a little.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So you add pumps that push the water hard, day and night.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Then one pipe bursts, and the whole city goes dry.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So you lay a second pipe and connect the two in a loop.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now water reaches every street from two directions.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And you add valves every few blocks.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "When a pipe breaks, you close two valves, and only one block loses water.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "But all that pressure finds every tiny crack in old pipes.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Water seeps out day and night and washes the soil away.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Until the street drops into a hole.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Mexico City loses thirty to forty percent of its drinking water to leaks like these.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So you put sensors that listen for leaks, and lower the pressure at night, when nobody's using it.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Congratulations! You just built a city water supply. What should we build next?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  }
 ],
